why 2 1/2 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano and tenor move from D/G to E/A a fifth apart, with the tenor dipping to D in between. Bach treats the tenor's leap as breaking up the fifths; in exercises they still count.
why 7 1/4 LEAPS The bass steps E–F and leaps up to B♭. The tritone E–B♭ spans two chords, and the B♭ belongs to a diminished seventh that moves on at once.
why 7 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps down a diminished seventh, B♭ to C♯, inside one diminished chord. The leap stays within the chord and the bass goes on by step to D, so the line holds together.
why 7 1/2 OVERLAP The tenor drops to A, below where the bass just was, because it takes over the bass's B♭ and resolves it. The bass has leapt far down, so the lines do not tangle.
why 10 3/4 CROSSING The tune leaps down from B to E, below the alto's G, at the start of a new phrase. Bach keeps the alto's G rather than moving it under the melody.
why 10 7/8 CROSSING The same beat: the soprano holds E under the alto's G; the alto falls to D on the next beat.
why 11 3/4 DIRECT The tune leaps up a fourth to G while the bass steps up to G: hidden octaves, softened by the step in the bass. The leap belongs to the hymn tune.
why 12 1/2 CADENCE The melody ends on E, so Bach closes on the E major chord, which in A minor is V: a Phrygian ending, common in chorales.
why 7 1/2 SEVENTH The B♭ is resolved, but in another voice: the tenor takes the A right where the bass leaves B♭, while the bass leaps down to C♯.
